WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:01.968
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.069
We're here with Taylor swift.

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.904
And we were talking
during the break.

00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:07.740
Some very exciting
collaborations that you are now

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.376
able to do because you're
re-recording these songs --

00:00:10.376 --> 00:00:12.879
Chris Stapleton, Ed Sheeran,
Phoebe Bridgers.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:14.346
Is that --
-Yeah.

00:00:14.346 --> 00:00:16.415
[ Cheers and applause ]
-It must be so nice to

00:00:16.415 --> 00:00:19.418
have a wish list, and then
reach out to these people.

00:00:19.418 --> 00:00:23.656
-It really, really was.
Essentially, there are songs

00:00:23.656 --> 00:00:27.227
on this album that I've called
"from the vault" tracks,

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:29.728
because in my mind,
there's a metaphorical vault

00:00:29.728 --> 00:00:33.333
of songs that I love
that I've written --

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:37.270
I write a lot of songs for
albums, and then I pare it down.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.139
But these were songs that
didn't make it on the album

00:00:40.139 --> 00:00:42.375
because I wanted to save them
for the next album.

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.143
And then, it turned out,
the next album was, like,

00:00:44.143 --> 00:00:46.880
a whole different thing,
and so they get left behind.

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.516
And you always think back on
these songs, and you're like,

00:00:49.516 --> 00:00:52.952
"What would have happened?
I wish people could hear this."

00:00:52.952 --> 00:00:55.421
But it belongs
in that moment in time.

00:00:55.421 --> 00:01:00.125
So now that I get to go back
and revisit my old work,

00:01:00.125 --> 00:01:06.032
I've dug up those songs
from the crypt they were in,

00:01:06.032 --> 00:01:09.769
and I have, like --
I've reached out to artists

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:12.604
that I love and said, "Do you
want to sing this with me?"

00:01:12.604 --> 00:01:15.809
You know, Phoebe Bridgers
is one of my favorite artists

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:18.144
in the world.
I just think she's --

00:01:18.144 --> 00:01:21.447
Like, if she sings it,
I will listen to it.

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:22.982
[ Laughter ]
It's --

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:25.418
I just love her voice,
and then I also love

00:01:25.418 --> 00:01:28.287
that she's a very funny person.
-Do these people believe it

00:01:28.287 --> 00:01:30.823
when they get a phone call,
saying, "Hi, it's Taylor Swift.

00:01:30.823 --> 00:01:31.991
I want you
to sing a song with me?"

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:33.626
-Well, I try not
to cold call people.

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.428
-Right.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:35.428 --> 00:01:37.196
That's a good instinct, yeah.
-It can go very bad.

00:01:37.196 --> 00:01:41.767
-Sure.
-But, you know, I do send, like,

00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:47.139
a very long text that
I've crafted over many days.

00:01:47.139 --> 00:01:49.008
And I'll send the song,
because I don't want them

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:51.310
to ever feel pressured
to say yes to something

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:54.179
creatively if it doesn't gel
with what they want to do.

00:01:54.179 --> 00:01:56.716
So, with Phoebe, I reached out,

00:01:56.716 --> 00:01:58.517
and I sent her this song
called "Nothing New,"

00:01:58.517 --> 00:02:02.021
which I wrote when I was 22.
And it's really, really

00:02:02.021 --> 00:02:04.624
special to me
because it was the first time

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:06.626
I was not a shiny new artist.

00:02:06.626 --> 00:02:10.462
I was on my fourth album,
and I felt --

00:02:10.462 --> 00:02:12.098
Like, I think this happens
to a lot of artists,

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:14.099
where they have
their breakthrough moment.

00:02:14.099 --> 00:02:16.369
And then, the moment after that
is really hard for them

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.004
because they're just
not getting the same --

00:02:19.004 --> 00:02:22.741
It's like the first time
you walk into a room at a party

00:02:22.741 --> 00:02:25.578
and everyone's like,
"Oh, Kevin's here!"

00:02:25.578 --> 00:02:28.014
When you have your breakthrough
moment, like, "Kevin!"

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:31.717
And then, the next time, you
walk in, they're like, "'Sup?"

00:02:31.717 --> 00:02:32.985
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.985 --> 00:02:35.521
-And then, the third time
you walk in, they're like,

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:39.091
"Um, so what do you want
to do tomorrow?"

00:02:39.091 --> 00:02:41.026
and they're just talking.
And you're like,

00:02:41.026 --> 00:02:42.828
"But I'm here,
and you used to --"

00:02:42.828 --> 00:02:45.431
So I was dealing with
that moment in my life,

00:02:45.431 --> 00:02:48.000
and so that song's
really special to me.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:51.704
And I sent it to Phoebe
and said it would mean

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:53.306
the world to me
if you would do this as a duet.

00:02:53.306 --> 00:02:54.873
'Cause I really wanted
another female artist

00:02:54.873 --> 00:02:57.543
who I loved to sing it with me,
because I think it has a very

00:02:57.543 --> 00:03:01.346
female-artist perspective, that
we go through that experience.

00:03:01.346 --> 00:03:03.816
And her response was,

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.585
"I've been waiting for this text
my entire life."

00:03:05.585 --> 00:03:06.986
[ Laughter ]

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:10.656
It was like, "Yes!"
-That's a very --

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:12.058
I'm sure
she was waiting for that.

00:03:12.058 --> 00:03:14.293
I think a lot of people
are waiting and excited

00:03:14.293 --> 00:03:16.095
for the fact
that you have recorded --

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:18.831
There's a 10-minute version
of a song --

00:03:18.831 --> 00:03:23.302
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:24.670
I mean,
I-I just want to say.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:25.738
-That was
a really nice reaction.

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:27.707
-I haven't heard a gasp
like that...

00:03:27.707 --> 00:03:29.142
-That was great.
-...since I said,

00:03:29.142 --> 00:03:30.376
"Now it's time
for 'A Closer Look.'"

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:35.481
-That was very powerful.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:37.517
-So, tell us about --
But this was --

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:39.118
So you've always
had this version.

00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:41.688
-Yes. This is the original
thing that I wrote.

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:44.857
So, there's a song called
"All Too Well"

00:03:44.857 --> 00:03:48.527
that was never a single.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:48.527 --> 00:03:50.095
You guys are really --

00:03:50.095 --> 00:03:52.031
This is very nice,
what you're doing out there.

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:53.765
[ Laughter ]

00:03:53.765 --> 00:03:56.435
This song was never a single,
it never had a video,

00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:59.071
and somehow,
the fans just turned it into

00:03:59.071 --> 00:04:01.440
the song from this album.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:04.209
And when I --
I used to get so sad

00:04:04.209 --> 00:04:05.645
when I would sing it
that I could barely, like,

00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:08.347
get through the song.
And then, over time, I realized

00:04:08.347 --> 00:04:11.417
the fans were just, like,
screaming the words back to me

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:14.853
so loudly that it made it
a very joyful experience for me

00:04:14.853 --> 00:04:16.322
to sing this song.
And since --

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:18.624
It has been a joyful thing
for me to sing.

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:20.893
I play it all the time
in concert.

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:24.230
And it's just so fun
to hear them scream the words.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.633
So this song originally was --

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:29.668
It's a very long song, as it is,

00:04:29.668 --> 00:04:32.272
but it was originally
10 minutes long.

00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:35.774
And I just kept --
I was just like --

00:04:35.774 --> 00:04:38.678
[ Laughter ]
I couldn't stop.

00:04:38.678 --> 00:04:41.514
But I was like, "Oh, you got to
fit something on an album,

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:44.516
so that's unreasonable for it
to be 10 minutes long."

00:04:44.516 --> 00:04:47.420
And so I had to cut out certain
verses and parts of the bridge

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.290
and lots of different things
that I really loved.

00:04:51.290 --> 00:04:54.760
Left some of my favorite lines
on the cutting-room floor,

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.963
and I'm really happy
that people get to hear them.

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:58.764
I'm so proud of
this version of it.

00:04:58.764 --> 00:05:00.799
I think this version
is the version of the song

00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:02.201
that was meant to be heard.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:07.974
-That is really thrilling.
And then, on top of all that,

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.743
there's a short film
that you wrote.

00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:12.044
-Yeah.
-That you directed.

00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:14.280
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:17.616
Two fantastic young actors --
Dylan and Sadie,

00:05:17.616 --> 00:05:19.618
who I believe --
I hope this is correct --

00:05:19.618 --> 00:05:20.987
also huge fans of yours.

00:05:20.987 --> 00:05:22.988
It must have been a big deal
for them to do this.

00:05:22.988 --> 00:05:24.490
-It was really wonderful

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:26.291
working with Sadie Sink
and Dylan O'Brien.

00:05:26.291 --> 00:05:27.993
They're just absolutely --

00:05:27.993 --> 00:05:31.564
I'm just blown away by what
they did in this short film.

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:36.969
I wrote this short film
and wanted to direct it,

00:05:36.969 --> 00:05:40.873
and the only two people
that I imagined playing

00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:44.343
the two characters --
it was Sadie and Dylan.

00:05:44.343 --> 00:05:46.746
If Sadie --

00:05:46.746 --> 00:05:48.213
She was the first one I went to.

00:05:48.213 --> 00:05:50.450
And if Sadie had said no,

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:51.884
I don't think
I would have made it.

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:53.118
I don't think
I would've made the film.

00:05:53.118 --> 00:05:54.152
I think I would've
just been like,

00:05:54.152 --> 00:05:56.289
"This is a sign,"
like, you know.

00:05:56.289 --> 00:06:00.459
But she --
I do like working with friends,

00:06:00.459 --> 00:06:04.096
or people who I think would be
excited about working with me.

00:06:04.096 --> 00:06:06.899
I don't really want
to have to convince someone.

00:06:06.899 --> 00:06:09.134
I'm not very interested
in that at all.

00:06:09.134 --> 00:06:12.004
If somebody -- I've never
made a short film before.

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:15.073
I've directed some music videos.
But I kinda needed to reach out

00:06:15.073 --> 00:06:18.077
to people who were like --
would maybe believe

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:22.047
that I would be capable of it,
and Sadie just --

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:24.884
Sadie got it immediately.
Dylan was like, "Yep."

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:27.653
And I watch everything he's in.
I'm a huge fan of his.

00:06:27.653 --> 00:06:30.890
And both of them, they just --
they went out,

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.792
and they just left it all on the
field there in that short film.

00:06:33.792 --> 00:06:36.996
They really just
acted their asses off.

00:06:36.996 --> 00:06:40.899
-Well, it's terrific.
And I can only imagine

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:43.669
what the next couple of days are
gonna be like for your fans.

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:44.970
You have done them --
-I hope good.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.071
I hope good --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:46.071 --> 00:06:50.075
-I feel very confidant
about that.

00:06:50.075 --> 00:06:51.777
It's so lovely to see you again.
-You're lovely.

00:06:51.777 --> 00:06:54.613
-Thanks for being here.
You guys, that's Taylor Swift.

00:06:54.613 --> 00:06:56.215
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:56.215 --> 00:06:58.918
"Red (Taylor's Version)"
is out tomorrow.

